Understand Rational Exponents

\textbf{A. What is the meaning of the exponent in the expression 16^{(1)/(4)}?}

Extend the properties of integer exponents to rational exponents.
\begin{align*}
16^{(1)/(4)} &= (2^4)^{(1)/(4)} Use the Power of a Power Property. 
&= 2^1 
&= \sqrt[4]{16} Since 2 = \sqrt[4]{16}, 16 = 2^4.
\end{align*}
Therefore, 16^{(1)/(4)} is another way to represent \sqrt[4]{16}, or 2.

In general, for positive integer n, x^{(1)/(n)} can be expressed as the radical \sqrt[n]{x}.
 x^{(1)/(n)} = \sqrt[n]{x} 
Both the exponent (1)/(n) and the radical symbol \sqrt[n]{x} indicate the principal nth root.

\textbf{B. Write 27^{(2)/(3)} as a radical.}
\begin{align*}
27^{(2)/(3)} &= (27^{(1)/(3)})^2 Use the Power of a Power Property. 
&= (\sqrt[3]{27})^2 
&= 3^2 
&= 9
\end{align*}
This means that you can rewrite 27^{(2)/(3)} as (\sqrt[3]{27})^2. Since 27 is a perfect cube, you can evaluate the radical expression mentally by taking the cube root first and then squaring.
 27^{(2)/(3)} = (\sqrt[3]{27})^2 = 3^2 = 9

Evaluate Expressions With Rational Exponents

\textbf{A. Evaluate the expressions 32^{(3)/(5)}, 27^{-(2)/(3)}, and 50^{(3)/(4)}.}

\begin{align*}
32^{(3)/(5)} &= (32^{(1)/(5)})^3 
&= 2^3 
&= 8
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
27^{-(2)/(3)} &= (27^{(1)/(3)})^{-2} 
&= (3)^{-2} 
&= (1)/(9)
\end{align*}

Since 50 does not have a perfect 4th root, use a calculator to approximate:
 50^{(3)/(4)} ≈ 18.80 

\textbf{B. The Fujita scale rating, F, of a tornado is represented by F = \sqrt[3]{((W)/(14.1))^2} - 2, where W is the estimated wind speed of the tornado in miles per hour. What is the Fujita scale rating for this tornado?}

[IMAGE: A tornado on a road, with label 'Wind speed estimated at 100 mph']

\begin{align*}
F &= \sqrt[3]{((100)/(14.1))^2} - 2 
&= ((100)/(14.1))^{(2)/(3)} - 2 
&≈ (7.09)^{(2)/(3)} - 2 
&≈ 3.69 - 2 
&≈ 1.69
\end{align*}

A tornado with estimated wind speeds of 100 mph is classified as F1 according to the Fujita scale.

